{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfwNjAdjkwcDK6tzfVWW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Dq.** In an octahedral d³ complex the lowest spin-allowed band is ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₂g, and its energy is exactly 10Dq. So 10Dq = 17 500 cm⁻¹, which gives Dq = 1750 cm⁻¹.\n\n**B.** The other two spin-allowed bands, ν₂ and ν₃, are the two ⁴T₁g levels. These come from a 2×2 secular problem, and their sum is ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B.\n\n- ν₂ + ν₃ = 23 860 + 37 910 = 61 770 cm⁻¹.\n- 30Dq = 52 500 cm⁻¹, so 15B = 9270 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 618 cm⁻¹.\n\nI checked this against the splitting of the two levels:\n\n- ν₃ − ν₂ = √(225B² + 100Dq² − 180B·Dq).\n- With B = 618 and Dq = 1750 this gives 14 054 cm⁻¹.\n- The observed difference is 14 050 cm⁻¹, so the fit is consistent.\n\n**C.** The sharp band is the spin-forbidden ⁴A₂g → ²Eg transition. I used the approximate expression E(²Eg) ≈ 9B + 3C − 90B²/(10Dq). I did not solve the full matrix.\n\n- 9B = 5562 cm⁻¹.\n- 90B²/(10Dq) = 90 × 381 924 / 17 500 ≈ 1964 cm⁻¹.\n- 12 090 = 5562 + 3C − 1964, so 3C = 8492 and C ≈ 2831 cm⁻¹.\n\nThis gives C/B ≈ 4.6, a reasonable value for a d³ ion such as Cr³⁺. The value of C depends on the approximate ²Eg formula, so it is less exact than Dq and B.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1750, \"B\": 618, \"C\": 2831, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq=ν1/10, B from ν2+ν3=30Dq+15B, C from the approximate 2E energy 9B+3C−90B²/10Dq.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":857,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":857,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fa1c9aa-7bd1-42a7-8a49-4b4b7f8d844c","uuid":"2b4f79d0-28d7-4072-ad40-8eb1f7ab2e6c","timestamp":"